 course today that  around 4.8 million people have had no  vaccinations at all and that remains a  significant problem for the government  that hesitancy that reluctance to come  forward and get any kind of protection  something that the prime minister did  mention and some of the language in that  statement too was i think particularly  stark if you like at the end there  essentially warning that this is the way  to protect our way of life it's not new  restrictions but it is a threat that  actually this is the first post in  trying to make sure that omicron doesn't  overwhelm the nhs doesn't make it really  difficult over this winter period people  now need to go and take up that offer  now we know that the nhs is going to  struggle to give that  to make that offer happen which is why  the military will be involved it's why  we expect there will be new walk-in  centers there'll be longer hours earlier  and later opening for people who work  but it will be all about reaching those  hard-to-reach groups going out into the  communities ensuring that everybody  understands this offer and takes it up  quickly that will be where this  protection really comes into its own and  without it i think the nhs will struggle  but the prime minister will struggle too  because if people don't get boosted if  people refuse to if they think it's not  important or it's not relevant to them  or you know it's only pretty mild it's  probably not going to affect me remember  this is not just about all of us it's  altruistic that's what vaccination is  about at the end of the day it's about  protecting everybody in society i think  if that message doesn't penetrate then  things could get quite difficult and  that's the point where you really start  to see that conversation about further  restrictions  uh as you're speaking kate we've had a  response from uh sikhistan leader of the  labour party to the prime minister's  statement he says it is more important  than ever to get britain boosted to  protect our nhs and save lives the  arrival of the new worrying variant is a  reminder that the pandemic is not over  and vaccines are the best weapon in our  fight he went on to say the vaccine is  safe and effective it protects us and  those around us will always support the  nhs and act in the public interest he  goes on to say we've come so far since  those darkest of days at the start of  the pandemic and then repeats that  message telling people to get boosted  and you mentioned the language that the  prime minister chose to use and  something struck me right at the end of  his statement he said get boosted now to  protect our nhs protect our freedoms and  our way of life do you think there was a  message there potentially to those tory  rebels who are so concerned about our  freedoms being restricted  i think there probably is i think  there's also a broader message to those  that group that i just mentioned there  who are unvaccinated the prime minister  was pretty stark when he was talking  about people who were unvaccinated  earlier on this month suggesting that  there could be a conversation about how  to ensure those people are vaccinated  but you're right to pick up on the  politics of this of course because the  measures that the prime minister has  introduced will need to be voted on this  week there are a rump of conservative  mps who do not like them they will not  vote for them and that's a growing group  and their concern is freedom they feel  the conservative party is about personal  choice it's about liberty it's about  freedom to choose how you behave and  they don't like this encroachment this  constant reaching for restrictions  that's how they characterize it of  course the prime minister is balancing  all of that political turmoil with the  turmoil that's going on in the nhs and  and the potential for that being  overwhelmed too those are two very very  difficult things to make  well to meet in the middle if you like  they're really 